    {
      "id": "CRD-02",
      "title": "From the shortest row to a real cusp parameter",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "anchor": "inputs-cusp-reduction",
      "statement": "The bottom diagonal norm B of the reduced upper triangular matrix is the height of its minimizing last row. A two-coordinate competitor yields B≤C√(AB) and hence B≤C²A for the preceding diagonal norm A. Induction gives all adjacent ratios. In real positive parameters the bound is C^(−2/d)r_(i+1)≤r_i.",
      "hypotheses": "Fix a number field L of degree d, rank N≥1 and an invertible adelic matrix. Rational row height is finite, invariant under rational scalar multiplication and right maximal compact factors. Compact representatives exist modulo L in the additive adèles and modulo L× in the norm-one idèles.",
      "definitions": [
        "projective adelic row height",
        "global idèle norm of a singleton diagonal entry",
        "the norm-one and common positive parts of the diagonal"
      ],
